WORLD BANK WATER GLOBAL PRACTICE
Lessons from Urban Water Supply and Sanitation Reform Operations
Global Water Security and Sanitation Partnership (GWSP) | 2023
Source: documents.worldbank.org | Report Series: Water Global Practice Knowledge Notes

INTRODUCTION:
This synthesis draws on lessons from 34 World Bank-supported urban WASH reform operations
completed between 2018-2023 across Sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, and the Middle East and
North Africa regions. Total portfolio value: approximately $4.2 billion.

KEY LESSON 1: TARIFF REFORM SEQUENCING IS THE SINGLE MOST CRITICAL DESIGN DECISION
Across the 34 operations reviewed, projects that attempted cost-recovery tariff increases before
demonstrating service quality improvements failed to achieve tariff reform objectives in 28 of 34 cases.
The political economy of water pricing is highly sensitive: communities that have experienced decades
of poor service will not accept higher prices without first experiencing improved service.

Recommended sequencing:
- Year 1-2: Service quality investments (pressure, reliability, quality)
- Year 2-3: Transparent communication of cost structure and subsidy levels
- Year 3-4: Phased tariff increases with lifeline tariff protection for lowest quintile
- Year 4-5: Full cost recovery trajectory established

Projects that followed this sequencing achieved cost recovery targets in 19 of 34 cases (56%),
compared to 2 of 34 (6%) for projects that attempted tariff reform first.

KEY LESSON 2: NON-REVENUE WATER REDUCTION REQUIRES ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS BEFORE INVESTMENT
The median NRW rate across the reviewed portfolio was 44% (range: 28%-67%). The common mistake
was investing in pipe replacement without first conducting a root cause analysis of NRW sources.
Root cause analysis across the portfolio found:
- Illegal connections: 45-65% of NRW in most urban systems
- Commercial losses (meter tampering, billing errors): 15-25%
- Real losses (pipe leaks): 20-35%

Targeting illegal connections through community engagement programs produced faster and cheaper
NRW reductions than physical pipe replacement in 22 of 34 projects. Engaging communities in
identifying illegal connections and offering formalization pathways was more effective than
punitive enforcement approaches.

KEY LESSON 3: WOMEN IN WATER GOVERNANCE IMPROVES SERVICE DELIVERY OUTCOMES
Projects that mandated minimum 30% female representation on Water User Committees or Utility
Oversight Boards showed measurably better outcomes across three dimensions:
(1) Complaint resolution rates: 47% higher in projects with gender-equitable governance
(2) Willingness to pay for improved services: 23% higher in communities with female committee leadership
(3) Hygiene behavior change outcomes: 34% higher adoption rates

The mechanism appears to be women's prioritization of water reliability for household health management
and their stronger accountability relationships within communities.

KEY LESSON 4: CROSS-MINISTERIAL COORDINATION IS THE MOST COMMON CAUSE OF IMPLEMENTATION DELAYS
In 29 of 34 projects, cross-ministerial coordination failures between the Ministry of Finance and
the sector ministry (MoW, MoPW, or equivalent) caused average delays of 5.8 months in the first year.
Common causes: budget release delays, hiring approvals, tariff approval authority disputes.

Mitigation measures that worked:
- Establishing a Project Steering Committee at Deputy Minister level from project effectiveness
- Pre-agreeing budget release mechanisms in legal covenants
- Including cross-ministerial coordination as a quarterly ISR reporting requirement

KEY LESSON 5: UTILITY REFORM FATIGUE IS REAL AND MUST BE MANAGED
In projects where utilities had undergone 3 or more previous reform programs, staff resistance
significantly slowed implementation in Year 1-2. Diagnostic interviews revealed that staff had
experienced previous reforms as threatening job security without improving working conditions.

Effective approaches:
- Conducting organizational culture assessments before project design
- Including staff engagement and change management budgets (recommended: 6-8% of TA component)
- Framing reforms around service delivery outcomes rather than institutional restructuring
- Celebrating early wins publicly with staff recognition

KEY LESSON 6: SUSTAINABILITY REQUIRES GOVERNMENT BUDGET INTEGRATION FROM DAY ONE
The most common cause of project outcomes reversal post-completion was failure to integrate
project-funded activities into government budgets before project close. In 18 of 34 projects,
key positions funded by the project (utility managers, M&E officers, maintenance crews) were
not absorbed into government payroll, causing effective capacity collapse within 18 months.

Recommendation: Projects should include a sustainability covenant requiring government budget
lines for project-funded positions to be established by Year 2, verified at Mid-Term Review,
and confirmed at project close as a condition of final disbursement.
